{"reason": "The baseline model is restricted to a single training cohort (2021-01-01) plagued by pandemic-era inspection anomalies and omits critical municipal emergency enforcement actions that directly signal severe building distress. Stacking multi-cohort temporal snapshots from 2020 and 2021 will double effective training volume and stabilize learning, while incorporating point-in-time vacate orders and emergency repair program (ERP) charges will directly expose the highest-risk Class C violation precursors to both LightGBM and TabGatedNet. Modifying feature engineering and the training pipeline provides the highest impact while preserving model architectures and the 2022 hold-out evaluation benchmark.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest emergency enforcement datasets (hpd_vacate_orders, hpd_hwo_charges, and hpd_omo_charges) and engineer point-in-time historical features including 1-year and all-time counts, recency in days, and residential unit intensities across cohorts. WHY: Class C violations reflect immediately hazardous conditions that trigger city-directed emergency heat/hot water repairs and vacate orders, making historical municipal interventions the strongest structural distress indicators. HOW: Load tables from GCS with standard 10-digit BBL formatting; inside build_point_in_time_dataset, aggregate records with event dates strictly prior to cutoff_date into counts and recency metrics; unify categorical levels across train, validation, and test splits with a shared CategoricalDtype to prevent indexing mismatches. Interface constraints: Preserve the function signature build_point_in_time_dataset(entities, cutoff_date, label_window_months=12) and maintain bbl and target columns with uniform feature schemas across all splits.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement multi-cohort temporal pooling by concatenating the 2020-01-01 and 2021-01-01 point-in-time training snapshots into a unified training set, while keeping the 2022-01-01 cohort as the strict temporal hold-out validation set. WHY: A single annual cohort (2021) has limited sample diversity and reflects COVID-19 municipal inspection distortions; pooling multi-year cohorts doubles positive supervision for rare emergency actions and stabilizes tree splits and neural attention weights. HOW: Concatenate 2020 and 2021 cohort DataFrames to form df_train, fit StandardScaler on the pooled numerical features, train TabGatedNet and LightGBM on the expanded dataset, track AP on the 2022 validation set, and output final rank-blended predictions. Interface constraints: Preserve tensor dataset structures, DataLoader batch pipelines, X_train/X_val/X_test variable definitions, and final submission formatting to ./submission/submission.csv."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The baseline model is restricted to a single training cohort (2021-01-01) plagued by pandemic-era inspection anomalies and omits critical municipal emergency enforcement actions that directly signal severe building distress. Stacking multi-cohort temporal snapshots from 2020 and 2021 will double effective training volume and stabilize learning, while incorporating point-in-time vacate orders and emergency repair program (ERP) charges will directly expose the highest-risk Class C violation precursors to both LightGBM and TabGatedNet. Modifying feature engineering and the training pipeline provides the highest impact while preserving model architectures and the 2022 hold-out evaluation benchmark.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest emergency enforcement datasets (hpd_vacate_orders, hpd_hwo_charges, and hpd_omo_charges) and engineer point-in-time historical features including 1-year and all-time counts, recency in days, and residential unit intensities across cohorts. WHY: Class C violations reflect immediately hazardous conditions that trigger city-directed emergency heat/hot water repairs and vacate orders, making historical municipal interventions the strongest structural distress indicators. HOW: Load tables from GCS with standard 10-digit BBL formatting; inside build_point_in_time_dataset, aggregate records with event dates strictly prior to cutoff_date into counts and recency metrics; unify categorical levels across train, validation, and test splits with a shared CategoricalDtype to prevent indexing mismatches. Interface constraints: Preserve the function signature build_point_in_time_dataset(entities, cutoff_date, label_window_months=12) and maintain bbl and target columns with uniform feature schemas across all splits.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Implement multi-cohort temporal pooling by concatenating the 2020-01-01 and 2021-01-01 point-in-time training snapshots into a unified training set, while keeping the 2022-01-01 cohort as the strict temporal hold-out validation set. WHY: A single annual cohort (2021) has limited sample diversity and reflects COVID-19 municipal inspection distortions; pooling multi-year cohorts doubles positive supervision for rare emergency actions and stabilizes tree splits and neural attention weights. HOW: Concatenate 2020 and 2021 cohort DataFrames to form df_train, fit StandardScaler on the pooled numerical features, train TabGatedNet and LightGBM on the expanded dataset, track AP on the 2022 validation set, and output final rank-blended predictions. Interface constraints: Preserve tensor dataset structures, DataLoader batch pipelines, X_train/X_val/X_test variable definitions, and final submission formatting to ./submission/submission.csv."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The baseline achieved validation AP = 0.73879 because it relied on only a single historical cohort (2021) and was dragged down by an underperforming tabular ResNet (0.73389 vs LightGBM 0.73942) paired with naive equal rank blending. Historical experiments demonstrate that gradient-boosted decision trees far outperform neural networks on heavy-tailed zero-inflated violation counts, and multi-temporal pooling provides substantial statistical power. We focus on data_processing_and_feature_engineering, model_design, and training_evaluation to integrate multi-cohort temporal pooling with cross-agency emergency distress signals, replace the ResNet with XGBoost, and optimize ensemble blend weights against validation AP.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Implement multi-temporal cohort training by stacking training cohorts from cutoffs 2020-01-01 and 2021-01-01, and engineer point-in-time municipal distress features from hpd_omo_charges, hpd_hwo_charges, and dob_violations. Training on a single cohort leaves models vulnerable to single-year enforcement variations, while municipal emergency repair charges and DOB structural violations provide direct orthogonal signals of hazardous building distress prior to Class C violations. Ingest hpd_omo_charges, hpd_hwo_charges, and dob_violations with BBL resolution via clean_bbl_series, engineer 1-year and all-time counts and per-unit rates strictly prior to each cutoff date in extract_features, and vertically concatenate the 2020 and 2021 cohorts into df_train_feat while keeping validation strictly at 2022-01-01. Maintain the extract_features(bbl_list, cutoff_date) and compute_labels(bbl_list, cutoff_date, violations_df) function interfaces, retaining the 'feat_' column naming convention without lookahead leakage.", "model_design": "Replace the underperforming tabular HousingRiskResNet with a high-capacity XGBoost classifier configured for histogram-based gradient boosting while retaining and fine-tuning the LightGBM classifier. Tabular neural architectures struggle to capture sharp step-function thresholds on skewed count features and underperformed LightGBM (0.73389 vs 0.73942), whereas XGBoost with histogram tree learning natively handles zero-inflated sparsity and creates complementary non-linear splits. Configure xgb_model_params with objective='binary:logistic', eval_metric='aucpr', tree_method='hist', learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=6, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.8, scale_pos_weight=2.0, and n_estimators=2500, aligning with lgb_model_params. Preserve standard estimator interfaces so both tree models consume raw unscaled feature matrices X_train_raw and output calibrated class probabilities via predict_proba.", "training_evaluation": "Refactor the training, validation, and inference pipeline to fit both LightGBM and XGBoost on X_train_raw with early stopping and optimize their rank ensemble blend weights directly against validation Average Precision. The naive 50/50 rank blend previously penalized performance (degrading AP from 0.73942 down to 0.73879); searching for the optimal blend weight guarantees that the ensemble strictly dominates or matches individual models on the target competition metric. Fit both models with 50-round early stopping on the 2022 validation holdout set, convert validation probabilities to rank percentiles, grid search the optimal blend weight w in [0.0, 1.0] with step 0.05 on validation AP, and apply the optimal weights to test rank percentiles. Preserve submission generation to submission/submission.csv with columns ['bbl', 'score'], verify that all 171,587 test lots are scored without NaNs or duplicates, and print the final validation AP."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The baseline achieved validation AP = 0.73879 because it relied on only a single historical cohort (2021) and was dragged down by an underperforming tabular ResNet (0.73389 vs LightGBM 0.73942) paired with naive equal rank blending. Historical experiments demonstrate that gradient-boosted decision trees far outperform neural networks on heavy-tailed zero-inflated violation counts, and multi-temporal pooling provides substantial statistical power. We focus on data_processing_and_feature_engineering, model_design, and training_evaluation to integrate multi-cohort temporal pooling with cross-agency emergency distress signals, replace the ResNet with XGBoost, and optimize ensemble blend weights against validation AP.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Implement multi-temporal cohort training by stacking training cohorts from cutoffs 2020-01-01 and 2021-01-01, and engineer point-in-time municipal distress features from hpd_omo_charges, hpd_hwo_charges, and dob_violations. Training on a single cohort leaves models vulnerable to single-year enforcement variations, while municipal emergency repair charges and DOB structural violations provide direct orthogonal signals of hazardous building distress prior to Class C violations. Ingest hpd_omo_charges, hpd_hwo_charges, and dob_violations with BBL resolution via clean_bbl_series, engineer 1-year and all-time counts and per-unit rates strictly prior to each cutoff date in extract_features, and vertically concatenate the 2020 and 2021 cohorts into df_train_feat while keeping validation strictly at 2022-01-01. Maintain the extract_features(bbl_list, cutoff_date) and compute_labels(bbl_list, cutoff_date, violations_df) function interfaces, retaining the 'feat_' column naming convention without lookahead leakage.",
    "model_design": "Replace the underperforming tabular HousingRiskResNet with a high-capacity XGBoost classifier configured for histogram-based gradient boosting while retaining and fine-tuning the LightGBM classifier. Tabular neural architectures struggle to capture sharp step-function thresholds on skewed count features and underperformed LightGBM (0.73389 vs 0.73942), whereas XGBoost with histogram tree learning natively handles zero-inflated sparsity and creates complementary non-linear splits. Configure xgb_model_params with objective='binary:logistic', eval_metric='aucpr', tree_method='hist', learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=6, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.8, scale_pos_weight=2.0, and n_estimators=2500, aligning with lgb_model_params. Preserve standard estimator interfaces so both tree models consume raw unscaled feature matrices X_train_raw and output calibrated class probabilities via predict_proba.",
    "training_evaluation": "Refactor the training, validation, and inference pipeline to fit both LightGBM and XGBoost on X_train_raw with early stopping and optimize their rank ensemble blend weights directly against validation Average Precision. The naive 50/50 rank blend previously penalized performance (degrading AP from 0.73942 down to 0.73879); searching for the optimal blend weight guarantees that the ensemble strictly dominates or matches individual models on the target competition metric. Fit both models with 50-round early stopping on the 2022 validation holdout set, convert validation probabilities to rank percentiles, grid search the optimal blend weight w in [0.0, 1.0] with step 0.05 on validation AP, and apply the optimal weights to test rank percentiles. Preserve submission generation to submission/submission.csv with columns ['bbl', 'score'], verify that all 171,587 test lots are scored without NaNs or duplicates, and print the final validation AP."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The existing pipeline relies on coarse multi-year aggregates (1y, 2y, 3y, 5y) that cannot distinguish between active enforcement actions and long-resolved infractions, while completely omitting short-term complaint acceleration, block-level physical decay, and evictions. Furthermore, applying an artificial positive class weight of 2.0 distorts leaf posterior calibration away from true Bayes probabilities, degrading borderline ranking where Average Precision is most sensitive. Refining data_processing_and_feature_engineering to capture multi-scale temporal windows, inspection cadence, spatial block risk, and eviction distress, coupled with unweighted loss calibration and strengthened regularization in model_design, directly targets these performance bottlenecks.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest the evictions table and expand point-in-time feature extraction to include short-term temporal violation windows (30d, 60d, 180d counts for Class C and total violations), distinct inspection visit frequencies (unique inspection dates in 1y), open violation persistence ratios (open C and total violations divided by cumulative violations), 30-day complaint velocity over the annualized baseline, block-level Class C violation density, and 1y/all-time eviction counts. WHY: Class C violations trigger statutory 30-to-90-day follow-up inspections, while unresolved backlogs, acute complaint spikes, evictions, and block-level physical decay provide strong predictive signals of imminent hazardous conditions that coarse annual counts miss. HOW: Ingest evictions from GCS, standardize BBLs, and parse execution dates using extract_date_col; inside extract_features, construct prior event masks strictly before cutoff_date, compute rolling aggregates and ratios via pandas groupby and map, calculate tax-block Class C sums using the 6-digit borough-block prefix, and assign clean feat_ prefixes with zero-imputation. Interface constraints: Preserve the function signatures of extract_features(bbl_list, cutoff_date) and compute_labels(bbl_list, cutoff_date, violations_df), maintaining uniform feature names across 2020, 2021, 2022, and 2023 cohorts.", "model_design": "WHAT: Reset scale_pos_weight from 2.0 to 1.0 (unweighted log-loss) across both LightGBM and XGBoost, and strengthen tree regularization by setting reg_alpha=2.0, reg_lambda=8.0, and min_child_samples=50 (min_child_weight=5.0 for XGBoost). WHY: Average Precision evaluates monotonic ranking across all entities, and unweighted cross-entropy preserves true empirical posterior ordering without artificially skewing borderline predictions, while tightened leaf regularization prevents overfitting on the expanded multi-scale feature set. HOW: Update lgb_model_params and xgb_model_params dictionaries directly with the new loss weighting and regularization hyperparameters while retaining learning_rate=0.03, colsample_bytree=0.8, and early stopping. Interface constraints: Maintain existing model dictionary names lgb_model_params and xgb_model_params, ensuring scikit-learn classifier API compatibility and preserving predict_proba output formats for downstream rank blending."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The existing pipeline relies on coarse multi-year aggregates (1y, 2y, 3y, 5y) that cannot distinguish between active enforcement actions and long-resolved infractions, while completely omitting short-term complaint acceleration, block-level physical decay, and evictions. Furthermore, applying an artificial positive class weight of 2.0 distorts leaf posterior calibration away from true Bayes probabilities, degrading borderline ranking where Average Precision is most sensitive. Refining data_processing_and_feature_engineering to capture multi-scale temporal windows, inspection cadence, spatial block risk, and eviction distress, coupled with unweighted loss calibration and strengthened regularization in model_design, directly targets these performance bottlenecks.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Ingest the evictions table and expand point-in-time feature extraction to include short-term temporal violation windows (30d, 60d, 180d counts for Class C and total violations), distinct inspection visit frequencies (unique inspection dates in 1y), open violation persistence ratios (open C and total violations divided by cumulative violations), 30-day complaint velocity over the annualized baseline, block-level Class C violation density, and 1y/all-time eviction counts. WHY: Class C violations trigger statutory 30-to-90-day follow-up inspections, while unresolved backlogs, acute complaint spikes, evictions, and block-level physical decay provide strong predictive signals of imminent hazardous conditions that coarse annual counts miss. HOW: Ingest evictions from GCS, standardize BBLs, and parse execution dates using extract_date_col; inside extract_features, construct prior event masks strictly before cutoff_date, compute rolling aggregates and ratios via pandas groupby and map, calculate tax-block Class C sums using the 6-digit borough-block prefix, and assign clean feat_ prefixes with zero-imputation. Interface constraints: Preserve the function signatures of extract_features(bbl_list, cutoff_date) and compute_labels(bbl_list, cutoff_date, violations_df), maintaining uniform feature names across 2020, 2021, 2022, and 2023 cohorts.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Reset scale_pos_weight from 2.0 to 1.0 (unweighted log-loss) across both LightGBM and XGBoost, and strengthen tree regularization by setting reg_alpha=2.0, reg_lambda=8.0, and min_child_samples=50 (min_child_weight=5.0 for XGBoost). WHY: Average Precision evaluates monotonic ranking across all entities, and unweighted cross-entropy preserves true empirical posterior ordering without artificially skewing borderline predictions, while tightened leaf regularization prevents overfitting on the expanded multi-scale feature set. HOW: Update lgb_model_params and xgb_model_params dictionaries directly with the new loss weighting and regularization hyperparameters while retaining learning_rate=0.03, colsample_bytree=0.8, and early stopping. Interface constraints: Maintain existing model dictionary names lgb_model_params and xgb_model_params, ensuring scikit-learn classifier API compatibility and preserving predict_proba output formats for downstream rank blending."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "Execution logs show that TabGatedNet and LightGBM currently achieve a validation AP of 0.72671 in just 6 minutes, but performance is capped by missing cross-agency structural distress indicators, lack of short-term complaint acceleration, and a naive static 50/50 rank blend. Historical experiments demonstrate that gradient-boosted trees excel on skewed municipal violation distributions, DOB violations provide orthogonal hazard signals, and metric-driven rank optimization prevents suboptimal model dilution. We focus on data_processing_and_feature_engineering, model_design, and training_evaluation to ingest DOB violations and acute 30-day complaint velocity, introduce an XGBoost classifier with histogram binning for depth-wise tree diversity, and grid-search the optimal convex rank blend directly maximizing validation Average Precision.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "In data_processing_and_feature_engineering, ingest dob_violations to construct 10-digit BBLs from boro, block, and lot, engineer point-in-time 1-year and all-time DOB violation counts along with recency in days, extract acute 30-day complaint counts and short-term acceleration ratios from hpd_complaints, and calculate zipcode-level historical Class C violation densities strictly prior to each cutoff date. Emergency Class C housing violations often stem from unresolved structural and system failures flagged by the Department of Buildings (DOB) and recent surges in acute tenant complaints that annual aggregates obscure. Implement this by reading dob_violations with issue dates, joining to df_base in build_point_in_time_dataset prior to cutoff_date, computing comp_30d and comp_velocity_30d = (comp_30d * 12.0) / (comp_1y + 1.0), and mapping zipcode-level mean 1-year Class C violation rates strictly prior to the cutoff. Maintain the build_point_in_time_dataset(entities, cutoff_date, label_window_months) interface, preserve existing numeric and categorical column handling, and guarantee zero future lookahead.", "model_design": "In model_design, introduce an XGBoost classifier configured with histogram-based binning (tree_method='hist') and depth-wise tree growth alongside the existing LightGBM classifier and TabGatedNet neural network. While LightGBM builds asymmetric leaf-wise trees and TabGatedNet maps continuous multi-head attention representations, depth-wise XGBoost provides a third distinct inductive bias that captures balanced feature interactions on tabular counts and resists overfitting. Specify xgb_model_params with objective='binary:logistic', eval_metric='aucpr', tree_method='hist', learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=6, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.8, scale_pos_weight=2.0, reg_alpha=0.1, reg_lambda=1.0, and n_estimators=1000. Preserve standard estimator parameter interfaces and ensure compatibility with LightGBM and PyTorch components by accepting aligned input feature matrices.", "training_evaluation": "In training_evaluation, train XGBoost on the training cohorts with early stopping against the 2022 validation set, generate predicted probabilities across all three models (LightGBM, XGBoost, TabGatedNet), and optimize their convex ranking weights via grid search directly on validation Average Precision. Fixed 50/50 weighting is fundamentally suboptimal because it assigns arbitrary equal weight to neural predictions that underperform tree models, whereas direct AP-driven rank blending mathematically maximizes the precision-recall curve. Implement a grid search over candidate weight triples (w_lgb, w_xgb, w_nn) summing to 1.0 with step 0.05 on normalized rank percentiles to select the optimal ensemble combination on the 2022 validation set, and apply these weights to test ranks. Maintain the output path at submission/submission.csv with exact columns ['bbl', 'score'], ensuring all 171,587 test lots are present without NaN values or duplicate entries."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "Execution logs show that TabGatedNet and LightGBM currently achieve a validation AP of 0.72671 in just 6 minutes, but performance is capped by missing cross-agency structural distress indicators, lack of short-term complaint acceleration, and a naive static 50/50 rank blend. Historical experiments demonstrate that gradient-boosted trees excel on skewed municipal violation distributions, DOB violations provide orthogonal hazard signals, and metric-driven rank optimization prevents suboptimal model dilution. We focus on data_processing_and_feature_engineering, model_design, and training_evaluation to ingest DOB violations and acute 30-day complaint velocity, introduce an XGBoost classifier with histogram binning for depth-wise tree diversity, and grid-search the optimal convex rank blend directly maximizing validation Average Precision.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "In data_processing_and_feature_engineering, ingest dob_violations to construct 10-digit BBLs from boro, block, and lot, engineer point-in-time 1-year and all-time DOB violation counts along with recency in days, extract acute 30-day complaint counts and short-term acceleration ratios from hpd_complaints, and calculate zipcode-level historical Class C violation densities strictly prior to each cutoff date. Emergency Class C housing violations often stem from unresolved structural and system failures flagged by the Department of Buildings (DOB) and recent surges in acute tenant complaints that annual aggregates obscure. Implement this by reading dob_violations with issue dates, joining to df_base in build_point_in_time_dataset prior to cutoff_date, computing comp_30d and comp_velocity_30d = (comp_30d * 12.0) / (comp_1y + 1.0), and mapping zipcode-level mean 1-year Class C violation rates strictly prior to the cutoff. Maintain the build_point_in_time_dataset(entities, cutoff_date, label_window_months) interface, preserve existing numeric and categorical column handling, and guarantee zero future lookahead.",
    "model_design": "In model_design, introduce an XGBoost classifier configured with histogram-based binning (tree_method='hist') and depth-wise tree growth alongside the existing LightGBM classifier and TabGatedNet neural network. While LightGBM builds asymmetric leaf-wise trees and TabGatedNet maps continuous multi-head attention representations, depth-wise XGBoost provides a third distinct inductive bias that captures balanced feature interactions on tabular counts and resists overfitting. Specify xgb_model_params with objective='binary:logistic', eval_metric='aucpr', tree_method='hist', learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=6, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.8, scale_pos_weight=2.0, reg_alpha=0.1, reg_lambda=1.0, and n_estimators=1000. Preserve standard estimator parameter interfaces and ensure compatibility with LightGBM and PyTorch components by accepting aligned input feature matrices.",
    "training_evaluation": "In training_evaluation, train XGBoost on the training cohorts with early stopping against the 2022 validation set, generate predicted probabilities across all three models (LightGBM, XGBoost, TabGatedNet), and optimize their convex ranking weights via grid search directly on validation Average Precision. Fixed 50/50 weighting is fundamentally suboptimal because it assigns arbitrary equal weight to neural predictions that underperform tree models, whereas direct AP-driven rank blending mathematically maximizes the precision-recall curve. Implement a grid search over candidate weight triples (w_lgb, w_xgb, w_nn) summing to 1.0 with step 0.05 on normalized rank percentiles to select the optimal ensemble combination on the 2022 validation set, and apply these weights to test ranks. Maintain the output path at submission/submission.csv with exact columns ['bbl', 'score'], ensuring all 171,587 test lots are present without NaN values or duplicate entries."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current pipeline suffers from severe temporal covariate shift because filtering violations on static 'Open' status distorts training cohorts from 2020 and 2021 where violations resolved before 2023 are falsely treated as closed at cutoff. Concurrently, the models lack multi-scale hazard acceleration, cross-agency DOB/ECB infraction data, and spatial empirical priors, while gradient-boosted trees over-index on raw cumulative counters. Modifying 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' to reconstruct true point-in-time backlogs, ingest DOB/ECB violations, and build spatial and velocity metrics, combined with tightened regularization in 'model_design', directly rectifies feature distribution shifts and sharpens Average Precision ranking.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Reconstruct true point-in-time active open violation backlogs using currentstatusdate, engineer multi-scale hazard velocity metrics (30d and 90d Class C/B counts relative to annualized baselines), ingest dob_ecb_violations for hazardous building infractions, and compute hierarchical spatial smoothing features at the tax-block and ZIP-code levels. WHY: Violation status in the lake reflects a static snapshot on 2023-01-01, causing earlier cohorts (2020, 2021) to severely undercount open violations at their respective cutoffs, while acute hazard acceleration, cross-agency DOB infractions, and spatial empirical Bayes priors provide vital signal for properties with sparse HPD inspection histories. HOW: Ingest currentstatusdate from hpd_violations to classify a violation as active at cutoff if inspectiondate < cutoff and (violationstatus == 'Open' or currentstatusdate >= cutoff); ingest dob_ecb_violations with clean_bbl_series to compute rolling 1y and all-time counts; compute tax-block (6-digit prefix) and ZIP-level Class C aggregations, and compute short-term velocity ratios. Interface constraints: Preserve the function signatures and contracts of extract_features(bbl_list, cutoff_date) and compute_labels(bbl_list, cutoff_date, violations_df), ensuring consistent feat_ prefixed column names across all cohorts.", "model_design": "WHAT: Strengthen leaf regularization, tighten tree complexity, and increase feature subsampling randomness across LightGBM and XGBoost by setting min_child_samples=80, min_child_weight=8.0, colsample_bytree=0.7, subsample=0.75, reg_alpha=3.0, and reg_lambda=10.0. WHY: Lots with massive lifetime violation counts disproportionately dominate early tree splits and pull leaf posterior probabilities away from true monotonic rank calibration, eroding top-decile precision and Average Precision. HOW: Update lgb_model_params and xgb_model_params dictionaries directly with the tighter regularization hyperparameters while maintaining learning_rate=0.03, unweighted binary log-loss (scale_pos_weight=1.0), and PR-AUC early stopping. Interface constraints: Retain existing model parameter dictionary names lgb_model_params and xgb_model_params, preserving scikit-learn estimator interfaces and predict_proba outputs for rank blending."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current pipeline suffers from severe temporal covariate shift because filtering violations on static 'Open' status distorts training cohorts from 2020 and 2021 where violations resolved before 2023 are falsely treated as closed at cutoff. Concurrently, the models lack multi-scale hazard acceleration, cross-agency DOB/ECB infraction data, and spatial empirical priors, while gradient-boosted trees over-index on raw cumulative counters. Modifying 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' to reconstruct true point-in-time backlogs, ingest DOB/ECB violations, and build spatial and velocity metrics, combined with tightened regularization in 'model_design', directly rectifies feature distribution shifts and sharpens Average Precision ranking.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Reconstruct true point-in-time active open violation backlogs using currentstatusdate, engineer multi-scale hazard velocity metrics (30d and 90d Class C/B counts relative to annualized baselines), ingest dob_ecb_violations for hazardous building infractions, and compute hierarchical spatial smoothing features at the tax-block and ZIP-code levels. WHY: Violation status in the lake reflects a static snapshot on 2023-01-01, causing earlier cohorts (2020, 2021) to severely undercount open violations at their respective cutoffs, while acute hazard acceleration, cross-agency DOB infractions, and spatial empirical Bayes priors provide vital signal for properties with sparse HPD inspection histories. HOW: Ingest currentstatusdate from hpd_violations to classify a violation as active at cutoff if inspectiondate < cutoff and (violationstatus == 'Open' or currentstatusdate >= cutoff); ingest dob_ecb_violations with clean_bbl_series to compute rolling 1y and all-time counts; compute tax-block (6-digit prefix) and ZIP-level Class C aggregations, and compute short-term velocity ratios. Interface constraints: Preserve the function signatures and contracts of extract_features(bbl_list, cutoff_date) and compute_labels(bbl_list, cutoff_date, violations_df), ensuring consistent feat_ prefixed column names across all cohorts.",
    "model_design": "WHAT: Strengthen leaf regularization, tighten tree complexity, and increase feature subsampling randomness across LightGBM and XGBoost by setting min_child_samples=80, min_child_weight=8.0, colsample_bytree=0.7, subsample=0.75, reg_alpha=3.0, and reg_lambda=10.0. WHY: Lots with massive lifetime violation counts disproportionately dominate early tree splits and pull leaf posterior probabilities away from true monotonic rank calibration, eroding top-decile precision and Average Precision. HOW: Update lgb_model_params and xgb_model_params dictionaries directly with the tighter regularization hyperparameters while maintaining learning_rate=0.03, unweighted binary log-loss (scale_pos_weight=1.0), and PR-AUC early stopping. Interface constraints: Retain existing model parameter dictionary names lgb_model_params and xgb_model_params, preserving scikit-learn estimator interfaces and predict_proba outputs for rank blending."
